---
title: First-Difference vs. Standard Loss for ODE Parameter Estimation
author: Arnav Kapoor
---

# When does fitting first differences help?

This benchmark addresses part of [SciMLBenchmarks.jl#28](https://github.com/SciML/SciMLBenchmarks.jl/issues/28):
comparing the *standard* trajectory-matching loss used throughout this repository's other
parameter estimation pages (`sum((model - data)^2)`) against a *first-difference* loss that
instead matches consecutive differences (`sum((diff(model) - diff(data))^2)`).
First-differencing is a classic time-series trick for removing an unknown, roughly-constant
*offset* between model and data (a satellite sensor's fixed calibration bias, an
unsubtracted baseline fluorescence level, etc.): differencing exactly cancels an additive
constant, at the cost of amplifying the variance of any random measurement noise already
present in the data (since `Var(y_t - y_{t-1}) = 2*Var(y_t) - 2*Cov(y_t, y_{t-1})`, which
for independent noise is `2` times the noise variance). This benchmark makes that trade-off
concrete by fitting the classic Lotka-Volterra model under two synthetic-data scenarios:
pure Gaussian measurement noise, and noise plus a constant additive sensor offset.

```julia
using OrdinaryDiffEq, Optimization, OptimizationBBO, OptimizationNLopt, ForwardDiff
using ModelingToolkit
using ModelingToolkitBase
using SciCompDSL
using ModelingToolkit: @mtkbuild, D_nounits as D, t_nounits as t
using Random, Statistics, LinearAlgebra, DataFrames, Plots
gr(fmt = :png)
```

## Model and synthetic data

```julia
@mtkmodel LotkaVolterraTest begin
    @parameters begin
        a = 1.5  # Growth rate of prey
        b = 1.0  # Predation rate
        c = 3.0  # Death rate of predators
        d = 1.0  # Reproduction rate of predators
    end
    @variables begin
        x(t) = 1.0
        y(t) = 1.0
    end
    @equations begin
        D(x) ~ a * x - b * x * y
        D(y) ~ -c * y + d * x * y
    end
end
@mtkbuild sys = LotkaVolterraTest()

u0 = [1.0, 1.0]
p_true = [1.5, 1.0, 3.0, 1.0]
tspan = (0.0, 10.0)
prob = ODEProblem(sys, u0, tspan, p_true)

dt = 0.1
tgrid = collect(0.0:dt:10.0)
sol_true = solve(prob, Tsit5(), saveat = tgrid, reltol = 1e-9, abstol = 1e-9)
data_clean = Array(sol_true)
```

Two synthetic observation scenarios, both derived from the same clean trajectory:

```julia
Random.seed!(1)
sigma = 0.2
noise = sigma .* randn(size(data_clean))
data_noisy = data_clean .+ noise                    # pure measurement noise

sensor_bias = [0.5, -0.3]                            # a fixed, unknown per-channel offset
data_biased = data_clean .+ noise .+ sensor_bias     # noise *and* a constant bias
```

```julia
plot(tgrid, data_clean', label = ["x (clean)" "y (clean)"], xlabel = "time",
    title = "Synthetic Lotka-Volterra data", legend = :outertopright)
scatter!(tgrid, data_noisy', label = ["x (noisy)" "y (noisy)"], markersize = 2)
scatter!(tgrid, data_biased', label = ["x (noisy+bias)" "y (noisy+bias)"], markersize = 2,
    markershape = :xcross)
```

## Objective functions

Both losses share the same forward simulation; they differ only in whether the raw
trajectory or its first difference along time is compared to the data.

```julia
function simulate(p)
    _prob = remake(prob, p = p)
    sol = solve(_prob, Tsit5(), saveat = tgrid, reltol = 1e-8, abstol = 1e-8)
    sol.retcode == ReturnCode.Success || return nothing
    return Array(sol)
end

function loss_standard(p, data)
    m = simulate(p)
    m === nothing && return 1e10
    return sum(abs2, m .- data)
end

function loss_firstdiff(p, data)
    m = simulate(p)
    m === nothing && return 1e10
    return sum(abs2, diff(m, dims = 2) .- diff(data, dims = 2))
end
```

```julia
lb = [0.1, 0.1, 0.1, 0.1]
ub = [5.0, 5.0, 5.0, 5.0]
init = [1.0, 1.0, 1.0, 1.0]

function fit(loss, data; maxiters = 5000)
    optf = OptimizationFunction((p, _) -> loss(p, data))
    optprob = OptimizationProblem(optf, init, lb = lb, ub = ub)
    res = solve(optprob, BBO_adaptive_de_rand_1_bin(), maxiters = maxiters)
    optprob2 = OptimizationProblem(optf, res.u, lb = lb, ub = ub)
    solve(optprob2, Opt(:LN_BOBYQA, 4), maxiters = 2000)
end
```

## Fitting both scenarios with both losses

```julia
res_std_noisy = fit(loss_standard, data_noisy)
res_fd_noisy = fit(loss_firstdiff, data_noisy)
res_std_biased = fit(loss_standard, data_biased)
res_fd_biased = fit(loss_firstdiff, data_biased)

df = DataFrame(
    scenario = ["noise only", "noise only", "noise + constant bias", "noise + constant bias"],
    loss = ["standard", "first-difference", "standard", "first-difference"],
    a = [res_std_noisy.u[1], res_fd_noisy.u[1], res_std_biased.u[1], res_fd_biased.u[1]],
    b = [res_std_noisy.u[2], res_fd_noisy.u[2], res_std_biased.u[2], res_fd_biased.u[2]],
    c = [res_std_noisy.u[3], res_fd_noisy.u[3], res_std_biased.u[3], res_fd_biased.u[3]],
    d = [res_std_noisy.u[4], res_fd_noisy.u[4], res_std_biased.u[4], res_fd_biased.u[4]],
    param_error = [
        norm(res_std_noisy.u .- p_true), norm(res_fd_noisy.u .- p_true),
        norm(res_std_biased.u .- p_true), norm(res_fd_biased.u .- p_true)])
df
```

## A cleaner isolation of the bias effect

The fits above mix noise and optimizer variability into the comparison. To isolate exactly
what first-differencing buys you, repeat the biased scenario with the noise switched off
entirely -- an additive bias with no other corruption:

```julia
data_bias_only = data_clean .+ sensor_bias
res_std_clean_bias = fit(loss_standard, data_bias_only)
res_fd_clean_bias = fit(loss_firstdiff, data_bias_only)

df_clean = DataFrame(
    loss = ["standard", "first-difference"],
    a = [res_std_clean_bias.u[1], res_fd_clean_bias.u[1]],
    b = [res_std_clean_bias.u[2], res_fd_clean_bias.u[2]],
    c = [res_std_clean_bias.u[3], res_fd_clean_bias.u[3]],
    d = [res_std_clean_bias.u[4], res_fd_clean_bias.u[4]],
    param_error = [norm(res_std_clean_bias.u .- p_true), norm(res_fd_clean_bias.u .- p_true)])
df_clean
```

With a pure constant bias and no noise, the first-difference loss recovers the true
parameters to solver precision (`diff` exactly cancels a per-channel constant added to
every time point), while the standard loss converges to a measurably wrong parameter
vector: a constant offset is not reproducible by shifting `(a, b, c, d)` alone, since it
changes the shape of the trajectory relative to the true dynamics, not just its level.

## Averaging over noise realizations

A single noisy fit is not a reliable comparison; repeat the pure-noise scenario over
several random seeds to see which loss is more accurate *on average* when there is no bias
to correct for.

```julia
seeds_range = 1:5

function repeat_seeds(loss; seeds = seeds_range, maxiters = 4000)
    errs = Float64[]
    for s in seeds
        Random.seed!(s)
        noise_s = sigma .* randn(size(data_clean))
        res = fit(loss, data_clean .+ noise_s; maxiters = maxiters)
        push!(errs, norm(res.u .- p_true))
    end
    return errs
end

errs_standard = repeat_seeds(loss_standard)
errs_firstdiff = repeat_seeds(loss_firstdiff)

df_seeds = DataFrame(
    seed = collect(seeds_range), standard_err = errs_standard, firstdiff_err = errs_firstdiff)
df_seeds
```

```julia
println("mean standard error:   ", mean(errs_standard))
println("mean firstdiff error:  ", mean(errs_firstdiff))
```

# Conclusion

The two losses trade off in exactly the way the noise-amplification argument predicts.
With pure, zero-mean measurement noise and no systematic offset, the *standard* loss gives
a lower average parameter error, since first-differencing doubles the effective noise
variance without buying anything in return. With a constant, unknown additive bias --
something differencing can remove exactly, but which the standard loss cannot explain away
by adjusting `(a, b, c, d)` alone -- first-differencing recovers the true parameters almost
exactly, while the standard loss converges to a biased answer. In practice, prefer
first-differencing when you have reason to suspect an unknown constant offset in your
measurements (an uncalibrated baseline, a fixed sensor drift over the observation window)
and your data is otherwise reasonably clean; prefer the standard loss for noisy but
well-calibrated data. Neither loss dominates unconditionally, so the right choice is a
modeling decision about what kind of imperfection your data actually has.

